.\" The manual for lib/cleanup-branches, which is what `git cleanup branches
.\" --help` opens: the script hands --help to man, since git passes arguments
.\" after a subcommand through untouched. Only `git cleanup --help` is git's,
.\" and is git-cleanup.1. -h prints the short form.
.\"
.\" What is common to every `git cleanup` subcommand (the flags, the file the
.\" editor opens, the exit status) is in git-cleanup.1, not repeated here.
.\"
.\" The list of words is written out three times: here, in the script's
.\" words(), which is also the header of the file the editor opens and what -h
.\" prints, and nowhere else. tests/test_cleanup_branches.sh fails when this
.\" page lacks one the script reads, so they cannot drift apart unnoticed.
.TH GIT\-CLEANUP\-BRANCHES 1 "" "dotfiles" "dotfiles"
.SH NAME
git\-cleanup\-branches \- delete local git branches that are merged or pushed and have been quiet for a week
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git cleanup branches
[\fB\-n\fR | \fB\-y\fR] [\fB\-\-days\fR \fIN\fR]
.br
.B git cleanup branches
\fB\-h\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sorts every local branch into a group, opens the list in your git editor, and
does what the saved list says. Only branches that have been idle for
.B \-\-days
days (seven unless told otherwise) start out turned on.
.PP
\fBbranch\fR and \fBbr\fR do for \fBbranches\fR. The options, the file the editor
opens and the exit status are the same for every subcommand and are in
.BR git\-cleanup (1).
.PP
Local branches only. Remote branches are never touched.
.PP
The threshold is a week and not the fourteen days of worktrees, because an idle
branch has nothing that is not already in the default branch or on a remote:
deleting it loses a name, and a single command brings that back. It is not
shorter because the local branch list is also a record of what was worked on
lately, and a branch merged on Monday should still be in it on Tuesday. A branch
with work that only it has is in \fBhas work\fR however old it is, so the
threshold cannot reach anything that matters.
.SH THE LIST
A branch is put in the first of these that fits it, and they are listed in this
order.
.TP
.B 1 protected
The default branch (what \fBorigin/HEAD\fR says, then \fBinit.defaultBranch\fR,
then \fBmain\fR or \fBmaster\fR), the branch you are on, and any branch that is
checked out in a worktree, which the row names. Never cleaned, and git refuses to
delete the last two itself. Written as a comment with no word, so it cannot be
turned on.
.IP
A worktree whose directory has gone still holds its branch until it is pruned,
and the row says so. Run \fBgit cleanup worktrees\fR first: removing a worktree
keeps its branch, which is then judged on its own the next time.
.TP
.B 2 locked
Marked as kept with \fBlock\fR. Shown with the reason. Written as \fB# unlock\fR.
.TP
.B 3 has work
Commits that are on neither the default branch nor any remote\-tracking branch,
and that no merged pull request accounts for (see below). Written as
\fB# clean\fR.
.TP
.B 4 recent
Nothing to lose, but active within the last \fB\-\-days\fR days. Written as
\fB# clean\fR.
.TP
.B 5 idle
Nothing to lose and quiet for \fB\-\-days\fR days. Written as \fBclean\fR, so it
is on.
.PP
Idle ones are listed oldest first, recent and has\-work ones newest first. The
rows say how many commits are only here, or whether the branch is \fBmerged\fR
or \fBpushed\fR, and show \fBupstream gone\fR when the remote branch has been
deleted. That is shown and not trusted: it is as true of an unmerged branch
deleted on the remote.
.SH MERGED
A branch has nothing to lose when any of these holds, checked in this order.
.IP \(bu 2
Its tip is reachable from the local default branch.
.IP \(bu 2
Every commit on it is on some remote\-tracking branch: it has been pushed.
.IP \(bu 2
A merged pull request has this branch's name \fIand\fR its tip commit. This is
for squash merges, which GitHub makes of most pull requests: the branch's own
commits never reach the default branch, only a new one holding the same change,
and \fBgit branch \-\-merged\fR cannot see that.
.PP
The tip is what stops a branch that went on after the merge, or was amended,
from being called merged: it stays in \fBhas work\fR.
.PP
The pull requests are asked about by branch name, in one GraphQL request through
\fBgh\fR for all the branches that need it (forty at a time, so a longer list is
several), and only when some branch is not merged or pushed. It costs one round
trip whatever the repository's history, and has no limit to fall off: an old
branch is found as readily as a new one. A listing of the last thousand merged
pull requests was tried first and took several seconds on a repository with a
long history. Without \fBgh\fR, without a network, or on a remote that is not
GitHub, the run says so once and such branches stay in \fBhas work\fR.
.SH THE WORDS
The first word of a line says what happens to the branch it names. A line
starting with
.B #
and a space is off: take the
.B #
and the space off to turn it on, put them back or delete the line to skip it.
.TP
.B clean
Delete the branch. Refused if it has commits that are neither merged nor
pushed.
.TP
.B force
Delete it even then, losing those commits.
.TP
.B unlock
Unlock it, then clean.
.TP
.B unlock\-force
Unlock it, then force.
.TP
.B lock
Lock it instead, so it is kept: it is listed as locked from then on, and a locked
branch is never cleaned until unlocked. Say why in double quotes between the word
and the name:
.RS
.PP
.nf
lock "release 1.4"  feat/x
.fi
.PP
The reason cannot contain a double quote. Without one the lock says it was made
by this command. A different reason for a branch that is already locked replaces
the old one.
.PP
Git has no lock on a branch. This one is a setting,
\fBbranch.\fIname\fB.cleanupLock\fR, whose value is the reason, and only
\fBgit cleanup\fR honours it: \fBgit branch \-D\fR takes no notice. It lives in
the repository's own config, moves with \fBgit branch \-m\fR, and is removed when
the branch is deleted.
.RE
.PP
There is no \fBdiscard\fR, which has nothing to apply to here.
.SH SAFETY
.IP \(bu 2
.B clean
never throws work away. Turning on a whole group is safe, because it refuses a
branch with commits that exist nowhere else, as does \fBunlock\fR. Only
.B force
and
.B unlock\-force
written on the line delete those.
.IP \(bu 2
Each line is looked at again just before it is acted on. A branch that has moved
to a riskier group while the editor was open, or that a worktree has since
checked out, is refused rather than deleted on out\-of\-date information.
.IP \(bu 2
A branch is deleted with \fBgit branch \-D\fR after that check. \fB\-d\fR judges
"merged" against whatever is checked out, and refuses a squash\-merged branch that
is fine to go.
.IP \(bu 2
Deleting a branch deletes its reflog, so every deletion prints the commit the
branch was at, \fBdeleted feat/x (was a1b2c3d)\fR. \fBgit branch feat/x a1b2c3d\fR
brings it back.
.IP \(bu 2
A lock is put back, with its reason, if a branch that was unlocked to be deleted
could not be.
.SH ACTIVITY
The newer of the tip commit's date and the newest entry in the branch's own
reflog, so a branch made today from an old commit is not idle. The reflog is
stamped when the branch is made and on a commit, reset or rebase on it, and not
on a checkout, so a branch that was only looked at reads as quiet. That costs
nothing: a quiet branch with no work of its own is what is being looked for.
.SH EXAMPLES
See what would happen, and change nothing:
.PP
.nf
git cleanup branches \-n
.fi
.PP
Delete what is idle without looking at it:
.PP
.nf
git cleanup br \-y
.fi
.PP
Treat a week as idle, and review the list:
.PP
.nf
git cleanup branches \-\-days 7
.fi
.PP
In the editor, delete a branch with unmerged work and keep another for good:
.PP
.nf
force  spike  origin/spike  40d  3 commits only here
lock "release hotfix"  hotfix/1.4  origin/hotfix/1.4  12d  pushed
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git\-cleanup (1),
.BR git\-cleanup\-worktrees (1),
.BR git\-branch (1)
